.TH FILLET 1
.SH NAME
fillet \- round the edges of solids
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fillet
[
.B \-l
]
.IR stream | \-
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fillet
reads a stream and rounds the edges of its solids as given by the
.B round
and
.B sharp
statements,
.IR part (5).
A round is the surface swept by a ball rolling along the edge
in contact with both faces
(a cylinder along a line, a torus along a circle, a pipe along an
ellipse, where a plane meets a cylinder at a slant, as a drafted face
meets a round one or a hole;
.IR stream (5)).
A round whose tube would fold over itself, its radius past that of the
curve of the center of its ball where it runs, is refused.
Larger radii are made first, and of one radius the concave rounds
before the convex ones, so that a convex round runs along a concave
one of its radius as along a larger one.
At a corner, rounds of one radius are continued along a smooth edge,
ended on a face, joined in threes by a sphere,
or mitered in pairs along an ellipse.
A round whose own face runs on smoothly into the face it ends on, as
a round along a floor edge that goes round a larger round into a wall,
ends on that face where its ball touches it; on a torus whose plane
touches its inner circle, the end is a nodal section of the torus,
.IR stream (5).
Where the two far faces of a miter meet the plane of the miter in two
lines, as across a crease, a piece of that plane closes the gap between
the two rounds up to the edge of the far faces; a gap of less than
1e-7 mm closes on the miter itself.
Where the crease turns the other way from the rounds, as where a
drafted face runs past a step, the far curves of touch reach the crease
before the plane of the miter; the round whose far curve meets the
miter first ends on it there, a sliver past the crease, and the other
ends on the crease, along the miter and then along the curve where it
meets the far face of the first, which closes the sliver.
A round is named by the names of its two faces joined by +,
a corner sphere by the names of its three.
.TP
.B \-l
List the edges instead, one per line
.RI ( "name name" " convex|concave|smooth" " angle length" ,
the angle in degrees, 0 when smooth).
.SH SEE ALSO
part(5), extrude(1), bool(1), mold(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The following are refused:
a rule that matches no edge, with the line of the rule after it that
takes all its edges if there is one;
any other kind of corner;
a radius with no room for a neighboring face or edge;
a radius larger than a round face it meets, as a corner of a drafted
profile that narrows toward an end, with that face and its radius at
the middle of the edge.
A corner is named by its place and radius, a radius by the two faces
of its edge.
.SH BUGS
A piece of a miter plane thinner than about 1e-6 mm, but not under
1e-7 mm, is exact but finer than OCCT can tell, which then finds its
edges crossing.
A nodal section has no exact STEP curve;
.IR tostep (1)
writes it as cubic B-splines that keep within about 1e-10 mm of it.
